\description{
The shape palettes made with \code{unicode = TRUE} draw each shape by asking the
device font for a Unicode character. Fonts draw those characters at unrelated
sizes, so a circle, a triangle and a plus sign from one font can differ by
a factor of two in area, and a font may not have the character at all.
\code{shapes_font()} registers a font that ships with ggthemes and contains the
geometric shapes, arrows, signs and marks used by the palettes, at the same
Unicode characters, so the same palettes draw with comparable sizes.
}
\section{Size}{
Each glyph is scaled until the convex hull of its outline has the area of
one reference circle. That keeps hollow, filled and rotated shapes
comparable, and it is the convention R's own \code{pch} symbols follow (circle,
square, diamond and triangle have equal area). Stroke widths are fixed. The
exclamation mark is too narrow to reach the reference area without outgrowing
the em box, so it is drawn smaller.
}

\section{Coverage}{
The font covers every shape the \code{unicode = TRUE} palettes draw, so they need
no other font: \code{\link[=calc_shape_pal]{calc_shape_pal()}}, \code{\link[=cleveland_shape_pal]{cleveland_shape_pal()}},
\code{\link[=stata_shape_pal]{stata_shape_pal()}} and \code{\link[=tableau_shape_pal]{tableau_shape_pal()}}. It also covers the Tableau
\code{"kpi"}, \code{"Arrows"} and \code{"Thin Arrows"} shapes and the sign shapes of
\code{"Gender"} in \code{ggthemes_data$tableau[["shape-palettes"]]}. It does not cover
the pictograph shapes: the Tableau \code{"weather"} and \code{"Bug Tracking"} palettes
and the restroom signs of \code{"Gender"}. Those characters still come from the
device font. Palettes with no \code{unicode} argument, such as
\code{\link[=tremmel_shape_pal]{tremmel_shape_pal()}}, use base \code{pch} symbols and need no font.
